description:
File: packages/swf/src/buttons.ts lines 196-257 and 305-361.

Bug: The two main iteration loops in compileButton() do not skip guide or folder layers, unlike every other place in the codebase that iterates layers.

Loop 1 (char ID pre-pass, lines 196-257):
  for (const layer of layers) {
    for (const frame of layer.frames) {
      // processes displayObjects from ALL layer types, including guide and folder

Loop 2 (ButtonRecord building, lines 305-361):
  for (const layer of layers) {
    for (const frame of layer.frames) {
      // same — no guard

In compile.ts and sprite.ts, every layer loop has:
  if (layer.type === 'guide') continue;
  if (layer.type === 'folder') continue;

Consequence: Any button symbol that has a guide layer in its timeline (used to show alignment or hit-area helpers) will have those guide objects included in the emitted DefineButton2 ButtonRecords. These objects appear as extra visual elements inside the button in Ruffle, corrupting the button appearance. Similarly, objects nested inside folder layers are processed twice — once via the folder 'frame' and potentially again when iterating the children — which can cause duplicate character definitions and wrong depth assignments.

Fix: Add the standard guards at the top of both for-loops in buttons.ts:
  if (layer.type === 'guide') continue;
  if (layer.type === 'folder') continue;

Acceptance: Author a button symbol with a guide layer containing a shape; compile to SWF; confirm Ruffle renders the button without showing the guide-layer shape in any button state.
